@app.cell(hide_code=True)
def _():
    mo.md(r"""
    There are several existing encoder models now that take some image embedding produced by one of the many modern media representation models ( DINOv2, being self supervised, is my favorite ), and predicts how strongly some patch of the [[Visual Cortex]] will respond to it. Notably, it is not an arduous process to reverse this; we can use it as a signal for steering a generative model for stimulus construction for maximally driving said patch.
    [@hendersonDiffusionbasedStimulusOptimization2026] does this using a diffusion model, and unlike my [[Maximizing Activation through Differentiable Content Consumption|experiments]] with [[TribeV2]] it has experimental evidence of stimulus candidates crossing the "Sim2Real" barrier here of generalizing to new participants.

    Their model, BrainDiVE, steers with a single model however. [@princeParametricNeuralControl2026] shows for macaques neuronal recordings that models with near identical performance for natural images face strong disagreement when you change objective to neuronal *control*. Having enjoyed both of these papers thoroughly, I wanted to see if this would happen within BrainDiVE as well, or if a diffusion model of this sort (unlike the purely encoding models tested by Prince et al.) would smooth the difference away.

    Thus I fit encoders for 7 vision models on the Natural Scenes Dataset (four people, eight visual regions), each generating images, then asking all their opinions on their peers. Purely models judging models here until final section comparison against brain recordings.
    """)
    return


